  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "The notation $x\\to -{2}^{-}$ means that $x$ approaches $-2$ *from the left*. In the same way, the notation $x\\to {3}^{-}$ means that $x$ approaches $3$ *from the left* while $x\\to {3}^{+}$ means that $x$ approaches $3$ *from the right*. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14227291/assets/37af0eeb28d271efa4bd8f296b8c530b82a7f368f82fa96e025695317b81519d.png)\n\nFrom the given graph, we see that:\n\n- As $x$ approaches $-2$ from the left, the values of $f(x)$ do not approach $\\infty .$ So, statement I is false.\n\n- As $x$ approaches $3$ from the left, the values of $f(x)$ increase, approaching $\\infty .$ So, statement II is true.\n\n- As $x$ approaches $3$ from the right, the values of $f(x)$ do not approach $-\\infty .$ So, statement III is false.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\".",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
